{"artifact_reads":[{"digest_matched":true,"path":".gnd-planning/milestones/code-refactoring/notes/code-refactoring-plan.md"}],"concern_count":13,"disposition":"revise","reviewer_id":"claude","schema_version":2,"summary":"The plan's structure is sound: pins come before moves, variant encoders are never merged, and there are rules for shims and source scans. But there are three problems with what it claims. First, RF0 is allowed to run now and rewrites public-launch's own authority ledger and the test that guards it. Second, the RF1 pins do not cover the checker, diff, safefs, assurance and synth behaviour that RF3, RF4 and RF6 change. Third, the RF-D2 gate stops at Phase 4 sign-off, but Phase 5 publication comes after it. Further hardening issues: the move-only commit rule conflicts with the monkeypatch rule, the budgets have no floor, audit numbers are hard-coded into criteria, some completion predicates cannot fail, and RF-D6 tightens behaviour inside a phase that is meant to preserve it."}
{"concern_id":"rf0-preempts-public-launch-ledger","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/code-refactoring/notes/code-refactoring-plan.md","location":"§4 RF0 lines 124-126; §3 lines 107-108; RF-R7 line 66","observation":"\"Record the unit handoff in `migration/units.json`, and update the test that currently requires every unit to be source-owned.\" and \"Under RF-D2, RF0 and RF1 may run now.\" Context: tests/test_public_distribution.py::test_curated_manifest_and_source_owned_ledger reads the public-launch milestone's migration/units.json and asserts `not units[\"authority_transfer\"] and not units[\"publisher_enabled\"]` and that every row is source-owned. That test file is also listed in public-launch b1/b2/b3-files.json. In the public-launch plan, authority transfer belongs to Phase 5, and reconciling units against a source high-water mark belongs to Phase 6 (public-transition)."}],"recommendation":"Do not let RF0 edit public-launch's units.json or its invariant test before public-launch Phase 4 sign-off and its own transfer phases. Two options: record the handoff in a code-refactoring record and leave the launch ledger untouched until public-launch authorizes the change, or gate this RF0 step behind RF-D2 the same way src/gnd edits are gated. State which milestone owns that ledger change.","relevance":"blocking","severity":"high","title":"RF0 'may run now' yet rewrites public-launch's authority ledger and its guarding test"}
{"concern_id":"rf1-pins-miss-moved-surfaces","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/code-refactoring/notes/code-refactoring-plan.md","location":"§4 RF1 lines 138-144","observation":"The pin list is \"the parser tree, help text and exit-code map ...; the exact bytes of every canonical-JSON variant ...; the three `model_digest` meanings; the two divergent primitive resolvers ...; the ISO-datetime parse sites; and one golden replay per session-contract version\". There is no pin for checker issue lists, their ordering or their messages, diff output, safefs results, assurance outputs, the synth oracle, or agent_cli process behaviour."},{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/code-refactoring/notes/code-refactoring-plan.md","location":"RF4 lines 187-188, RF6 lines 220-221, RF4/RF6 criteria","observation":"\"Have `diff` call checker and admission directly instead of going through `api`\" is a re-route, not a move, and \"replaces the if/elif chains\" can reorder issues. Neither RF4-C* nor RF6-C* includes \"RF1 pins unchanged\", although RF2-C1 and RF5-C1 do."}],"recommendation":"Extend RF1 with corpus pins over checker verdicts, issue order and messages, diff output, safefs results, assurance and synth-oracle outputs, and agent_cli exit and stream behaviour. Add \"all RF1 pins unchanged\" as a named criterion to RF3, RF4, RF6 and RF8.","relevance":"blocking","severity":"high","title":"RF1 pins do not cover the behaviour RF3/RF4/RF6 change, so 'behavior-preserving' cannot be shown"}
{"concern_id":"rf-d2-gate-ends-before-publication","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/code-refactoring/notes/code-refactoring-plan.md","location":"§3 lines 107-108; §6 line 273; RF-D2 line 327","observation":"\"RF2 and later wait until public-launch Phase 3 acceptance and the Phase 4 candidate sign-off are recorded\" and \"No refactor commit lands between a release candidate and its sign-off.\" Context: public-launch Phase 5 (Publish) follows Phase 4, and P5-C1 requires that the \"public repository/license/docs match the approved candidate\". Phase 5 also inspects \"the whole reachable target history\"."}],"recommendation":"Extend the gate through Phase 5 publication, or require that publication target a recorded commit on a release branch, with refactor commits landing only off that branch. Also state that refactor commits enlarge the Phase 5 history inspection.","relevance":"hardening","severity":"high","title":"RF-D2 sequencing stops at Phase 4 sign-off, but publication comes after it"}
{"concern_id":"rf-d6-tightening-in-preserving-phase","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/code-refactoring/notes/code-refactoring-plan.md","location":"RF3 lines 170-172; RF-D6 line 331; §6 line 275","observation":"\"plus one primitive resolver, whose `inherits_reference` behavior is decided from the RF1 pin under RF-D6\". RF-D6 proposes to \"Keep `popvalidate`'s strict `is True` semantics\". Changing truthy callers to `is True` changes verdicts in RF3, a phase §3 calls behavior-preserving. RF7, the tightening phase, is deferred."}],"recommendation":"In RF3, keep both semantics behind an explicit variant parameter pinned per variant (predicate 3). Move any switch from truthy to strict into the deferred RF7 or a separately ruled decision with its own red-before-green case.","relevance":"hardening","severity":"medium","title":"RF-D6 default changes resolver behaviour inside a behavior-preserving phase"}
{"concern_id":"move-only-vs-monkeypatch-repoint","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/code-refactoring/notes/code-refactoring-plan.md","location":"RF-R4 line 63, RF-R5 line 64","observation":"\"Every move lands as a move-only commit (re-exports included) before any edit to the moved code\" while \"Re-exports do not carry monkeypatches across: every `monkeypatch` of a moved name is re-pointed at the new location in the same phase.\" A move-only commit therefore leaves tests that patch the old path silently ineffective, or red, until a later commit."}],"recommendation":"Require each move commit to re-point the monkeypatch targets and widen the source scans it affects, and require every commit to be green. Alternatively, define the move-only unit as move + test re-point.","relevance":"hardening","severity":"medium","title":"Move-only commit rule leaves monkeypatch tests broken between commits"}
{"concern_id":"budgets-unbounded","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/code-refactoring/notes/code-refactoring-plan.md","location":"RF2-C2 line 165, RF3-C2 line 179, RF5-C3 line 209, RF5 residual lines 212-213, RF6-C1 line 225, §6 line 276","observation":"The targets are \"reach the spec's target\", \"falls by at least the spec's target\" and \"at most the line budget the spec declares\". \"If so, the spec splits RF5 into numbered parts\". The plan sets no floor, so a target of zero passes, the number of parts is unbounded, and there is no milestone-level stop."}],"recommendation":"State minimum directional targets in the plan (for example: merged-family duplicates = 1, a core private-import reduction > 0, a cli.py ceiling). Cap the number of RF5 parts, or require an owner gate before each additional part. Add a milestone-level stop condition.","relevance":"hardening","severity":"medium","title":"Phase budgets are delegated to specs with no floor and RF5 can split without limit"}
{"concern_id":"unverified-audit-numbers-in-criteria","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/code-refactoring/notes/code-refactoring-plan.md","location":"line 16; thesis line 21; RF2 line 156; RF4-C3 lines 193-195; §9 line 311","observation":"\"No other audit claim was re-proved\", yet the plan states as fact \"a 13-module import cycle\", \"Merge the 11 identical canonical-bytes sites\", \"the 47 private imports from `synth.boundary`\" and \"the 12 files that patch `gnd.cli`\". This contradicts RF0-C3: \"counted at execution time, not copied from the audit\"."}],"recommendation":"Mark each number as an unverified audit claim to be replaced by the RF0 measurement, and phrase RF4-C3 and the other criteria in terms of the RF0-measured count.","relevance":"hardening","severity":"medium","title":"Criteria and steps hard-code unverified audit counts"}
{"concern_id":"unfalsifiable-predicates","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/code-refactoring/notes/code-refactoring-plan.md","location":"completion predicates 2 and 4, lines 37-38 and 43-45","observation":"\"a test fails if a lower-layer module imports a higher layer\": the plan never defines a module-to-layer map. \"no command handler holds domain logic that `api.py` or a lane `service.py` cannot also reach\": \"domain logic\" has no measurable definition."}],"recommendation":"Commit a declared layer map in RF1, used by the xfail layering test. Restate predicate 4 measurably, for example: command modules import only from api or service modules, plus an allow-list, as checked by the RF0 tool.","relevance":"hardening","severity":"medium","title":"Layering and thin-CLI predicates cannot fail as written"}
